<h1>Transfer Conveyors: Right-Angle, 90° &amp; Multi-Directional</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A transfer conveyor changes the direction of product flow between two conveyor lines, typically at a 90-degree (right-angle) intersection. Transfer conveyors include pop-up chain transfers, pop-up ball transfers, right-angle roller chain transfers, turntables, and push diverters. Each type handles different product sizes, weights, and throughput requirements, making transfer technology selection critical for efficient <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line layouts</a> and distribution system routing.</p>
<h2 id="types-heading">Five Transfer Conveyor Technologies</h2>
<h3>Pop-Up Chain Transfer</h3>
<p>Pop-up chain transfers use parallel chains mounted between the rollers of a <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyor</a>. A pneumatic or electric actuator lifts the chains above the roller surface. The chains engage the product bottom and carry it perpendicular to the original direction of travel. When the transfer is not active, the chains retract below the rollers and the product continues straight on the roller conveyor.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/transfer-roach-cdlr-dual-chain-transfer.webp" width="1000" height="750" alt="Two roller conveyor sections on white frames with orange chain guards, each with chain strands set between the rollers and running across the roller direction." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Chain transfers built into Roach chain-driven live roller conveyor sections. The chain strands sit between the rollers and move the load at 90 degrees to the roller line. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://roachconveyors.com/conveyor_solutions/specialty_conveyors/specialty_transfers/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Roach Conveyors</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<p>Cycle time is the sum of lift, cross-transfer travel, and lower, so it depends on product size, chain speed, and actuator type. The product keeps its orientation throughout the transfer. Load ratings range widely: case-handling units carry cartons and totes, while heavy-duty pallet units are a different class. Hytrol's <a href="https://cdn.hytrol.com/Pallet-Handling-Brochure.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">pallet handling brochure</a> lists chain transfers from the CT-200 (200-pound pallet) to the CT-6000 (6,000 lbs). Pop-up chains work best with flat-bottomed cases and cartons that span at least two chain strands.</p>
<h3>Pop-Up Ball Transfer</h3>
<p>Pop-up ball transfers use a grid of ball transfer units (omnidirectional ball bearings) mounted between rollers. When the balls rise above the roller surface, the product rides on the ball field and can be pushed or guided in any direction, not limited to 90 degrees. Pop-up ball transfers suit light products and are used where multi-directional routing is needed from a single transfer point.</p>
<p>Ball transfers provide the most routing flexibility but the least positive drive. They rely on external pushers, guide rails, or downstream conveyors to pull the product in the desired direction. They are slower than chain transfers and less suitable for high-throughput applications.</p>
<h3>Right-Angle Roller Chain Transfer</h3>
<p>A right-angle roller chain transfer is a dedicated section with perpendicular chain-driven rollers that transfer product at 90 degrees. Unlike pop-up units that lift above existing rollers, the right-angle transfer section has its own set of rollers oriented at 90 degrees to the incoming line. The product enters on the infeed rollers, stops, and the perpendicular rollers engage to carry the product sideways onto the discharge conveyor.</p>
<p>Right-angle chain transfers handle heavy loads, with pallet-rated units in the thousands of pounds (see the Hytrol CT series above), and provide positive drive throughout the transfer. <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR conveyor</a> systems commonly use right-angle chain transfers for <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet routing</a>.</p>
<h3>Turntable Transfer</h3>
<p>A turntable transfer rotates the entire conveyor section (and the product on it) to change direction. The product enters the turntable section, the section physically rotates 90, 180, or any angle, and the product exits in the new direction. Turntables span manual and powered designs. Hytrol's <a href="https://hytrol.com/products/accessories/turn-tables/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">turntable product page</a> lists a manually operated turntable rated at 600 lbs and a powered 26-CRPT rated at 4,000 lbs, and its pallet brochure lists flat-plate MDPT and HDPT turntables at 3,000 and 6,000 lbs. Turntables usually have the slowest cycle of the transfer types because the section must rotate and stop before the product moves on.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/transfer-alba-turntable-roller-deck.webp" width="800" height="500" alt="A round blue turntable on a blue stand, carrying a roller conveyor deck with a motor in a yellow guard at one side." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>An Alba pallet turntable with a roller deck. The deck takes a pallet in, rotates, and sends it out in a new direction. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.albamfg.com/blog/why-alba-turntables-stand-apart-in-heavy-duty-pallet-handling/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Alba Manufacturing</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<p>Turntable transfers excel in pallet handling where pop-up mechanisms cannot support the load. They also serve applications where the product must be rotated (not just redirected), for example, rotating a pallet 90 degrees for stretch wrapping or rotating a case for label application on a different face.</p>
<h3>Push Diverter</h3>
<p>A push diverter uses a pneumatic or electric actuator to push the product off the main conveyor line onto a perpendicular spur. The simplest designs use a flat pusher plate. More sophisticated versions use angled paddles or sweep arms that guide the product at an angle rather than a sudden perpendicular push. Push diverters suit light to moderate product weights. Maximum line speed and product weight depend on actuator force and stroke speed, so get both from the supplier.</p>
<p>Push diverters usually cost less than pop-up transfers but can rotate the product during the push, which is unacceptable for orientation-sensitive products. They also generate more product impact force. Push diverters are common in <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject systems</a> where failed QC products are pushed off the main line.</p>
