<p>Load ratings and cycle times are model-specific. Ask each supplier for the rated load and the cycle time with your product. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">cost guide</a> for pricing factors.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Transfer Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Packaging line routing:</strong> <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">Packaging lines</a> use transfers to route products between parallel process lines. Cases divert from a main transport line to one of several case packers, labelers, or palletizers using pop-up chain transfers. The PLC routes each case based on product SKU, destination, or load-balancing logic.</p>
<p><strong>Pallet handling:</strong> <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">Pallet conveyor systems</a> use right-angle chain transfers and turntables to route loaded pallets between stretch wrappers, labelers, and shipping lanes. Heavy pallet loads require positive chain drive or turntable rotation. <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR conveyors</a> feed right-angle chain transfers for heavy pallet routing.</p>
<p><strong>Distribution center sortation:</strong> Transfer conveyors divert totes and cartons from trunk lines to pick zones, pack stations, and shipping lanes. High-volume operations use shoe sorters or pop-up wheel and swivel-roller diverters. As one example, Interroll's <a href="https://www.interroll.com/fileadmin/products/product_data/RM-8711/RM8711_EN.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">High Performance Divert (HPD) datasheet</a> lists a 0.3-second swivel time per 90 degrees and a 50 kg maximum load. Lower-volume operations use pop-up chain transfers or push diverters.</p>
<p><strong>Reject and quality control:</strong> <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">Reject systems</a> use push diverters to remove failed products from the main conveyor line. Checkweighers, metal detectors, and vision systems trigger the diverter to push rejects onto a spur conveyor or into a reject bin. Push diverters have a short, single-stroke cycle, which suits reject applications.</p>
<p><strong><a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">Collating and laning:</a></strong> Transfer conveyors distribute products from a single infeed lane across multiple output lanes. Each transfer diverts a programmed number of products to fill lanes evenly before <a href="/conveyor-functions/tray-loading/">tray loading</a> or case packing.</p>
<p><strong>Vertical integration:</strong> Transfer conveyors connect horizontal conveyor runs to <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">vertical reciprocating conveyors (VRCs)</a> and spiral conveyors. A right-angle transfer feeds product onto the VRC platform or spiral infeed, and another transfer receives product at the upper level.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">How to Select a Transfer Conveyor</h2>
<p><strong>Product weight drives the first cut.</strong> Light cases and totes can use pop-up chains, pop-up balls, or push diverters. Heavier cases need pop-up chain or right-angle chain transfers. <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">Pallet loads</a> use heavy-duty right-angle chain transfers or turntables. Match the transfer's rated load to your heaviest product, not the average.</p>
<p><strong>Throughput determines cycle time requirements.</strong> Calculate the maximum number of transfers per minute needed. Divide 60 seconds by that number to get the maximum allowable cycle time, including product travel on and off the transfer. Example: 12 transfers per minute allows 5 seconds per cycle. Compare that to the supplier's rated cycle with your product. If throughput exceeds single-transfer capacity, add parallel transfers or switch to sortation technology.</p>
<p><strong>Orientation sensitivity matters.</strong> Push diverters can rotate products during the push. Pop-up chain transfers and right-angle transfers maintain product orientation. Turntables intentionally rotate the product. If barcode labels, product graphics, or downstream equipment require a specific product orientation, avoid push diverters.</p>
<p><strong>Direction flexibility.</strong> Pop-up chains and right-angle transfers handle 90-degree transfers only. Pop-up ball transfers and turntables handle any angle. Push diverters typically push at 90 degrees, but angled sweeps and paddles can divert at shallower angles.</p>
<p>Pop-up mechanisms, turntables, and pushers create pinch and shear points. Specify guarding to <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a>, the ASME safety standard for conveyors and related equipment. Transfers must also integrate with upstream <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation zones</a> that meter products into the transfer at the correct spacing and timing. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyor zones</a> upstream of the transfer provide precise product positioning for reliable transfer operation.</p>
