<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a transfer conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A transfer conveyor changes product direction between two conveyor lines, typically at 90 degrees. Types include pop-up chain, pop-up ball, right-angle chain, turntable, and push diverter. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/">complete conveyor type guide</a> for the full picture.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is the difference between a pop-up transfer and a push diverter?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Pop-up transfers lift chains or balls above the roller surface to carry the product sideways. Push diverters physically push the product off the line. Pop-ups maintain orientation and handle higher loads. Push diverters usually cost less and cycle faster but may rotate the product.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How fast can a transfer conveyor operate?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Push diverters and swivel-roller diverters cycle fastest (Interroll lists 0.3 seconds per 90 degrees for its HPD swivel). Pop-up chain transfers are slower because they lift, carry, and lower. Turntables are slowest. Divide 60 seconds by your required transfers per minute to get the longest allowable cycle, then compare it to the supplier's rated cycle with your product.</p>

</div>
</div>
